---
import ComparisonPage from '../../../components/ComparisonPage.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../utils/paths';
import { GOLF_GPS_SITE_URL } from '../../../data/site';
import {
  APP_STORE_SEARCH_API_URL,
  COMPARISONS_CHECKED_ON,
  GOLF_GPS_ADVANTAGES,
  GOLF_GPS_TABLE,
  GPS_RIVALS,
  SWINGU_PRICING_URL,
  listingUrl,
} from '../../../data/comparisons';
import { GOLF_GPS_COMPARE_CALLS } from '../../../data/storeCalls';

const BIRDIES_URL: string = listingUrl(GPS_RIVALS.birdies);
const GOLFSHOT_URL: string = listingUrl(GPS_RIVALS.golfshot);
const HOLE19_URL: string = listingUrl(GPS_RIVALS.hole19);
const SWINGU_URL: string = listingUrl(GPS_RIVALS.swingU);
const GOLFLOGIX_URL: string = listingUrl(GPS_RIVALS.golfLogix);
---
<ComparisonPage
  pageTitle="Free Golf GPS Apps Compared for iPhone | SundayStroke"
  description="SundayStroke Golf GPS next to 18Birdies, Golfshot, Hole19, SwingU and GolfLogix: no ads on the free plan, free front, center and back yardages, a free layup target and the smallest download. Sourced and dated."
  heading="Free golf GPS apps compared"
  intro="Six golf GPS apps for iPhone that are free to download: ours, SundayStroke Golf GPS, and the five that come up most when golfers search. Free to download doesn't mean free to use, so this page looks at what you actually get on the free plan, and where Golf GPS comes out ahead. Every competitor fact links the page it came from."
  advantages={GOLF_GPS_ADVANTAGES}
  table={GOLF_GPS_TABLE}
  checkedOn={COMPARISONS_CHECKED_ON}
  methodSourceUrl={APP_STORE_SEARCH_API_URL}
  calls={GOLF_GPS_COMPARE_CALLS}
>
  <h2>No ads between you and your yardage</h2>
  <p>
    On the course you open a GPS app dozens of times a round, usually with a club in your other hand. An ad at that moment costs
    you time and focus. Golf GPS has no ads at all, on the free plan or with Pro.
  </p>
  <p>
    Elsewhere, ad-free play is something you pay for. Golfshot lists an ad-free experience among its Pro features
    (<a href={GOLFSHOT_URL}>Golfshot listing</a>), and Hole19 lists it as a Premium feature (<a href={HOLE19_URL}>Hole19 listing</a>).
    SwingU (<a href={SWINGU_URL}>listing</a>) and GolfLogix (<a href={GOLFLOGIX_URL}>listing</a>) show ads on their free plans too.
  </p>

  <h2>Front, center and back of the green, free</h2>
  <p>
    The three numbers most golfers want on every approach are the front, the center and the back of the green. The front tells
    you what you need to carry, the back what you can't go past, and the center is the safe number when the pin is tucked. Golf
    GPS shows all three for free, on a satellite map of the hole, in yards or meters.
  </p>
  <p>
    Golfshot puts front, center and back-of-green distances in its Pro membership
    (<a href={GOLFSHOT_URL}>Golfshot listing</a>). Pro plans there start at $4.99 and run to a $99.99 bundle, with a 7-day trial.
  </p>

  <h2>Plan a layup with a movable Target</h2>
  <p>
    On a par 5 or a long par 4, the useful question is often "where should I leave this?" rather than "how far is the green?".
    In Golf GPS you drag the Target anywhere on the map. The app shows two numbers: how far you are from the Target, and how
    far the Target leaves you from the green. Put it short of a bunker, read both numbers, and pick the club that leaves your
    favorite wedge in. That's included in the free plan.
  </p>

  <h2>A scorecard that's quick to fill in</h2>
  <p>
    You enter each hole's strokes with one tap of plus or minus, and swipe to change holes. Your total, your score against par
    and your front-nine and back-nine totals update on their own. Every finished round is saved to your history, and you can
    share it as an image with the group chat. All of it is free. Choose your tee before you start and the app uses that tee's
    course rating and slope for your round.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read <a href={sitePath('/golf-gps/scorecard/')}>how the Golf GPS scorecard works</a> for a hole-by-hole walkthrough.
  </p>

  <h2>Small download, ready when signal isn't</h2>
  <p>
    Golf GPS is a 52 MB download. The others in the table take 173 MB (SwingU), 220 MB (Golfshot), 246 MB (Hole19), 311 MB
    (18Birdies, <a href={BIRDIES_URL}>listing</a>) and 559 MB (GolfLogix). If your phone is full of swing videos, that difference
    matters. Golf GPS also runs on iOS 15.1 and later, where 18Birdies needs iOS 16, Golfshot iOS 17, and Hole19 and GolfLogix
    iOS 18, according to their listings.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two more things help on courses with patchy coverage. Data for the courses you played recently stays on your phone, so it
    is there when the clubhouse wifi isn't. When the GPS signal is weak, the app tells you and shows how far off the distances may
    be, instead of showing a number you shouldn't trust.
  </p>

  <h2>What the paid tiers cost elsewhere</h2>
  <p>Golf GPS keeps yardages, the Target, the scorecard and your round history free. For reference, the other apps list these paid tiers on the US App Store:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>18Birdies:</strong> $19.99 a month, $99.99 a year or $7.99 a week (<a href={BIRDIES_URL}>listing</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Hole19:</strong> Premium at $12.99 a month, $69.99 a year or $6.99 a week, and an Intelligence tier at $99.99 a year (<a href={HOLE19_URL}>listing</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Golfshot:</strong> Pro options from $4.99 to $79.99 and a Champions bundle at $99.99 (<a href={GOLFSHOT_URL}>listing</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>SwingU:</strong> Plus at $59.99 a year, Pro at $99.99 a year or $24.99 a month (<a href={SWINGU_PRICING_URL}>SwingU help</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>GolfLogix:</strong> Plus at $12.99 or $59.99; the listing doesn't show the billing periods (<a href={GOLFLOGIX_URL}>listing</a>).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Golf GPS Pro is optional. It adds an estimated handicap index and its trend, stats and trends across rounds, and shot tracking:
    mark each shot on the map to see how far it went, with your average and longest tracked shot. The handicap is an estimate
    built the way the World Handicap System works, from your tee's rating and slope and your lowest differentials; it is not an
    official Handicap Index. Our <a href={sitePath('/golf-handicap/')}>handicap guide</a> explains the math.
  </p>

  <h2>Golf GPS is a good fit if you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>want front, center and back yardages without a subscription;</li>
    <li>don't want ads on the course;</li>
    <li>like to plan layups with a target you can move;</li>
    <li>want a simple scorecard that saves every round;</li>
    <li>are short on phone storage or use an older iPhone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions golfers ask</h2>
  <h3>Does free to download mean every feature is free?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Most golf GPS apps are free to download and then keep some features for a paid tier. Compare what the free plan includes.
    In Golf GPS that's yardages to the front, center and back, the Target, the scorecard, round history and sharing, with no ads.
  </p>
  <h3>Does Golf GPS work in meters?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Switch between yards and meters in the app.</p>
  <h3>Do I have to find my course?</h3>
  <p>No. Golf GPS finds nearby courses automatically when you open it.</p>
  <h3>Is the Golf GPS handicap official?</h3>
  <p>No. Golf GPS Pro shows an estimate for tracking your game. For an official Handicap Index, join a club or your national golf association.</p>

  <p>
    See the whole app on the <a href={sitePath('/golf-gps/')}>Golf GPS page</a>, or visit its own site at
    <a href={GOLF_GPS_SITE_URL}>golfgps.ai</a>. Filming your round too? Compare <a href={sitePath('/compare/tracer/')}>golf ball tracer apps</a>.
  </p>
</ComparisonPage>
